<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/meeting.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/meeting-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/meeting.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal process</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Process guide</p><h1>How Insurance Appraisal Works in Michigan</h1><p class="ph-lede">Eight stages, from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what, and where Michigan claims most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-maize" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-glass" href="/request-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>8</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Award</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><p>When a Michigan property claim stalls over money, the appraisal clause in your policy gives both sides a structured way to settle the number without a lawsuit. This guide walks through each stage the way it usually unfolds, from the first spreadsheet comparison to the check in your mailbox. It explains the general framework in Michigan’s Insurance Code, but every policy is worded a little differently, so keep your own policy open as you read. We are appraisers, not lawyers, and nothing here is legal advice.</p>
<h2>Before you start</h2>
<p>Appraisal answers one question: how much is the loss worth? It doesn’t decide whether a loss is covered, whether an exclusion applies, or whether the insurer handled the claim fairly. If your insurer has denied the claim outright, appraisal usually isn’t the right tool, and a conversation with a Michigan attorney makes more sense. If the insurer agrees the loss is covered but its estimate is far below what contractors are quoting, appraisal is often a good fit.</p>
<p>Gather the basics first: the full policy with the declarations page and endorsements, every estimate the insurer has sent, your contractor bids, photos and video from the day of the loss, receipts for emergency mitigation, and any letters about depreciation or partial denials. Write down key dates, especially the date of loss and the date you reported it. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/appraisal-readiness-score/">appraisal readiness score</a> can help you check whether your file is ready.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Watch the clock</b>Michigan policies generally carry a one-year suit limitation, tolled while the claim is under review. Appraisal does not automatically pause every deadline, so ask a Michigan attorney how the timing applies to your claim.</div>
<h2>Stage 1: Document the gap in numbers</h2>
<p>A demand for appraisal is strongest when you can show exactly where the two estimates differ. Line up the insurer’s estimate against your contractor’s bid by room or by trade: roofing, drywall, flooring, electrical, contents. Mark each item as a quantity dispute (how many squares of shingles, how many square feet of drywall), a pricing dispute (the unit cost for the same work), or a scope dispute (whether an item belongs in the estimate at all). Scope disputes sometimes edge into coverage questions, so flag them early.</p>
<p>In an illustrative example, a Kalamazoo homeowner might find that the insurer allowed 22 squares of roofing while two contractors measured 31, and that the insurer left out drip edge and ice and water shield. That kind of gap is precisely what appraisal is built to resolve.</p>
<h2>Stage 2: Read the appraisal clause</h2>
<p>Michigan’s Insurance Code, at MCL 500.2833, requires fire and property policies issued in the state to include an appraisal provision. In general terms, it says that if the insured and the insurer fail to agree on the actual cash value or the amount of loss, either one may make a written demand for appraisal. Your policy repeats this in its own words, usually in the conditions section. Read it carefully for anything about how the demand must be sent, who pays the appraisers and umpire, and whether the policy addresses replacement cost holdbacks. Policy wording controls the details, so don’t assume another person’s policy matches yours.</p>
<h2>Stage 3: Send a written demand</h2>
<p>The demand should be in writing, name the claim number and date of loss, state that the parties disagree on the amount of loss, and invoke the appraisal provision. Send it in a way you can prove, such as certified mail plus email to the adjuster. Keep the tone factual. Some insurers respond by asking for more documents or by reopening negotiations, which is fine as long as the deadlines in the clause are respected. If you’d like help preparing the demand or a review before you send it, see our <a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> service.</p>
<h2>Stage 4: Each side names an appraiser</h2>
<p>Once a demand is made, each side selects a competent and independent appraiser. The statute generally sets a 20-day window after the demand for each side to name its appraiser and notify the other. “Independent” matters: an appraiser with a financial stake in the outcome, such as a contractor who would do the repairs, invites a challenge to the award. Ask any appraiser you’re considering about prior relationships with the insurer, how fees are structured, and whether they have handled the type of loss you have, whether that’s hail on a Grand Rapids ranch or a sewer backup in a Dearborn bungalow.</p>
<h2>Stage 5: Appraisers select an umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on an umpire, generally within 15 days. The umpire is a neutral who only steps in on items the appraisers can’t settle. If the appraisers can’t agree on a name, either side can ask a court of record in Michigan to select one. That adds time and some expense, so most appraisers work hard to agree. Good umpire candidates have a construction or estimating background and no ties to either side. We also serve as <a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/neutral-umpire-services/">neutral umpires</a> on claims where we have no other role.</p>
<h2>Stage 6: Inspect and exchange estimates</h2>
<p>The appraisers inspect the property, often together, and gather evidence: measurements, moisture readings, photos, material samples, invoices and code requirements from the local building department. Each appraiser prepares a detailed estimate, usually in Xactimate or a similar program, using pricing for the area. A roof in Marquette and a roof in Troy may price differently for labor and access. The two estimates are exchanged and compared item by item, and many disputed lines settle once both sides see the same facts.</p>
<h2>Stage 7: Resolve the remaining items</h2>
<p>Whatever the appraisers can’t agree on goes to the umpire. Each appraiser submits a short written position with supporting documents for each open item. The umpire may visit the property, ask questions, or request more information, then decides each item. Umpires generally don’t split every difference down the middle; they look for the better-supported figure. The fewer items left at this stage, the faster and cheaper the process tends to be.</p>
<h2>Stage 8: Sign the award and get paid</h2>
<p>An award in writing signed by any two of the three (the two appraisers, or one appraiser and the umpire) determines the amount of loss. A well-drafted award states the replacement cost value and actual cash value, often broken out by coverage part such as dwelling, other structures and contents, so payment is easy to calculate. The award doesn’t rule on coverage, penalty interest or attorney fees.</p>
<h2>After the award</h2>
<p>Once the award is signed, the insurer generally pays the actual cash value portion, minus your deductible and anything it already paid. Many Michigan homeowner policies hold back recoverable depreciation until repairs are completed or under contract, then release it when you send proof. Read the policy’s replacement cost conditions for any time limit on completing repairs. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV, ACV and holdback calculator</a> shows how those pieces fit together.</p>
<p>If you have a mortgage, expect the check to name your lender too, and expect the lender to manage disbursement for larger repairs. Keep invoices and photos of completed work so the holdback release goes smoothly.</p>
<p>Timing of payment also matters. Michigan’s Uniform Trade Practices Act, MCL 500.2001 and following, prohibits unfair claim settlement practices, and MCL 500.2006 provides for penalty interest of 12% per year on certain claims that aren’t paid on time. Whether that interest applies, and from what date, depends on the facts and on how courts read the statute, so it isn’t a guarantee. Appraisers don’t decide it. If you think payment is late or the insurer has acted unfairly, talk with a Michigan attorney, and know that you can also file a complaint with the Michigan Department of Insurance and Financial Services (DIFS).</p>
<p>Questions about any stage? Browse our <a href="/michigan-appraisal-questions/">appraisal FAQ</a> or <a href="/request-claim-review/">request a free claim review</a>.</p></article>
